<div class="wrap prose">
  <p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/guides/">Guides</a> &rsaquo; Writing essays</p>
  <h1>How to write an architecture essay or dissertation</h1>
  <p class="lead muted">Architecture is a design discipline, but you still have to write &mdash; &amp; a clear, argued essay is a skill markers reward as highly as a good drawing.</p>

  <p>Whether it is a short history &amp; theory essay or a final-year dissertation, academic writing in architecture follows the same logic as any humanities subject: make an argument &amp; support it with evidence. What changes is that your evidence includes buildings, drawings &amp; images, not only text. Get the structure right &amp; the writing follows.</p>

  <h2>Start with a question &amp; an argument</h2>
  <p>The strongest essays answer a specific question rather than surveying a topic. Narrow &quot;modernism&quot; down to something arguable: &quot;How did Le Corbusier&apos;s use of the pilotis change the relationship between building &amp; ground?&quot; Your answer to that question is your thesis &mdash; a single, contestable claim that every paragraph then works to support. If a marker cannot state your argument in one sentence after reading, it is not clear enough.</p>

  <h2>Structure</h2>
  <table class="data"><tr><th>Section</th><th>Job it does</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Introduction</td><td>Sets the question, states your thesis &amp; maps the essay</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Body paragraphs</td><td>One idea each, building the argument step by step</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Case studies</td><td>Specific buildings as evidence, analysed not just described</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Conclusion</td><td>Draws the threads together &amp; answers the question</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>Each body paragraph should make a point, support it with evidence (a building, a quotation, a drawing), analyse why that evidence matters, &amp; link back to the thesis. A dissertation scales this up with a literature review &amp; a methodology, but the underlying pattern is the same.</p>

  <h2>Build an argument, not a description</h2>
  <p>The most common weakness in student writing is description masquerading as analysis. Telling the reader that a building has a glass facade is description; explaining what that facade does &mdash; how it changes the experience, what it argues about transparency or technology &mdash; is analysis. Always push past &quot;what&quot; to &quot;so what&quot;. Marks live in the interpretation.</p>

  <h2>Referencing</h2>
  <p>Cite every idea, quotation &amp; image that is not your own. Most schools use a standard system such as Harvard (author-date) or footnotes; check your programme handbook &amp; stay consistent throughout. Keep full details as you go &mdash; author, title, year, publisher, page &mdash; because reconstructing a bibliography the night before a deadline is misery. Reference managers like Zotero automate this &amp; are worth learning early. Accurate referencing is also your defence against any accusation of plagiarism.</p>

  <h2>Using images</h2>
  <p>Images are evidence, so treat them as you would a quotation. Number &amp; caption every figure, credit its source, &amp; refer to it explicitly in the text (&quot;as Figure 3 shows&quot;) &mdash; never drop a picture in without discussing it. Choose images that make a point you are arguing, whether that is your own diagram, a plan or a photograph. A well-annotated drawing you have made yourself is often more persuasive than a borrowed photo.</p>

  <h2>Common pitfalls</h2>
  <table class="data"><tr><th>Pitfall</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Too broad a topic</td><td>Narrow to a single arguable question</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Describing, not analysing</td><td>Ask &quot;so what?&quot; of every observation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Unsupported claims</td><td>Back each point with a source or a building</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Images left uncredited</td><td>Caption, credit &amp; discuss every figure</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Leaving writing to the end</td><td>Draft early; strong writing needs revision</td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="callout"><strong>Tip.</strong> Write your introduction last. Once the essay exists you know exactly what you argued, so the opening can promise precisely what the body delivers.</div>
</div>
